.TH ADIN 1 "October 2026" "ADIN 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
adin \- offline-first cryptographic and encoding inspector
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B adin
.RI [ options ]
.I input
.br
.B adin
.BR -f " " \fIPATH\fR
.br
.I command
.B | adin -
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ADIN
(Automated Cryptographic Inspector) is an offline-first command-line tool that inspects strings and byte sequences for common encodings, cryptographic container and token formats, hash-shaped values, binary signatures, and heuristic classical-cipher candidates.
.PP
ADIN ranks possible interpretations and presents supporting evidence, confidence, decoded previews where appropriate, alternative matches, and limitations. It does not claim to identify every possible format. Many encodings overlap; ciphertext and random bytes often cannot be uniquely identified by appearance alone.
.PP
All analysis runs locally. ADIN does not use an AI model, contact a network, execute input or decoded content, crack password hashes, or decrypt unknown ciphertext.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -h , --help
Show the command-line help and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Show the ADIN version and exit.
.TP
.BR -f , --file \fIPATH\fR
Read exact bytes from the specified file. ADIN does not modify the file.
.TP
.B --json
Print a machine-readable JSON report instead of the normal terminal report.
.TP
.B --verbose
Show all ranked candidates and additional technical information, including evidence, parameters, statistics, and limitations.
.TP
.B --depth \fIN\fR
Set the maximum nested decoding depth from 0 to 10. The default is 3. A value of 0 disables nested decoding.
.TP
.B --max-bytes \fIN\fR
Set the maximum input size in bytes. The default is 1048576 (1 MiB). Values must be positive.
.SH INPUT
.I input
is one literal argument. Quote values that contain spaces or shell metacharacters. Alternatively, use --file to analyze a file, or pass a hyphen (\fB-\fR) to read standard input. If standard input is redirected or piped and no input argument is provided, ADIN reads from standard input.
.SH INTERPRETING RESULTS
.TP
.B Encoding
Changes how data is represented. Common encodings can often be reversed without a secret key. Encoding is not encryption.
.TP
.B Hash candidate
A digest is generally one-way. Digest length and character set alone rarely prove which algorithm produced it.
.TP
.B Encrypted format candidate
A structural marker may suggest a format used for encrypted data, but does not prove the payload is authentic or decryptable.
.TP
.B Confidence
Indicates how well an input matches a detector's expected pattern. It is not a probability of cryptographic validity.
.PP
A high entropy score does not by itself prove that data is encrypted. When formats are ambiguous, inspect the alternatives and limitations or treat the result as inconclusive.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Decode a hexadecimal string:
.B adin '48656c6c6f'
.TP
Inspect Base64 and nested encodings:
.B adin --verbose 'U0dWc2JHOD0='
.TP
Inspect a file:
.B adin --file ./evidence.bin
.TP
Read from a pipeline:
.B printf '%s' 'SGVsbG8=' | adin -
.TP
Produce JSON:
.B adin --json 'SGVsbG8='
.TP
Limit nested decoding:
.B adin --depth 1 'U0dWc2JHOD0='
.TP
Show available options:
.B adin --help
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Analysis completed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments or input-selection error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/bin/adin
User-local launcher installed by install.sh.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/man/man1/adin.1
User-local manual page.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/adin
Bash completion definition.
.TP
